  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 224 AM Thursday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Widespread rain and gusty winds, with embedded \nstorms in the east, will come to and end late this morning to early \nafternoon. \n\nSatellite imagery reveals the strong storm system in full force \nearly this morning. A pair of strong shortwaves exist at the moment, \nthe parent one over the MS valley and a secondary one over northeast \nFL. The latter system will continue to push northward into our area \nlater this morning and afternoon and eventually up into the northern \nMid-Atlantic as the parent trough tracks east into the Mid-Atlantic \ntonight and early Friday. At the surface, low pressure currently off \nthe coast of southeastern NC will track into central NC this morning \nto early afternoon before racing into New England tonight.\n\nLatest observations indicate that the northeasterly wind gusts have \nranged from 40 to 45 mph, with sporadic gusts to 50 mph, highest in \nthe Triad. We expect these wind gusts to continue until mid-morning. \nThus, the Wind Advisory will remain in place until its expiration at \n10 am. Once the surface low approaches later this morning and passes \nthis afternoon, the pressure gradient relaxes, allowing gusts to \ndramatically weaken into mid-afternoon to 15-25 mph.\n\nAs for rainfall observations so far, we have seen a smattering of \n0.5 to 1.5 inches of rain, with pockets of 2 to 3 inches over the \nTriangle and Coastal Plain. The highest rainfall totals are \npresently over the Coastal Plain. Much of the guidance is continuing \nto indicate that between now and sunrise, our highest rainfall rates \nare set to occur, when the intense moisture transport continues to \nlift north ahead of a warm front and the attendant surface low. \nUpstream observations northwest of Morehead City indicate some 1-2 \nin/hr rain rates presently. The REFS is indicating this may reach \nportions of the Flood Watch area until 12-13z, with pockets of 2-5 \ninches of additional rainfall. So far, our rivers have behaved, but \nthis additional rain could lead to isolated instances of flash \nflooding, especially in urban and poor drainage areas. As such, the \nFlood Watch will remain in place until 10am. Most high-res guidance \nshows the precipitation shield should lift into VA by the early \nafternoon as the dry slot lifts in from the southwest. Most rain \nwill come to an end sooner for all but our far northeast. The \nHRRR/RRFS continue to indicate some isolated showers and storms \ndeveloping along the warm front over the northern Coastal Plain from \nmid-morning until early afternoon, roughly between 8 am and 1 pm. \nDuring this time, a period of ~ 500 MLCAPE may combine with \nfavorable deep-layer shear upwards of 65-kt to support isolated \nsevere gusts or even an isolated tornado. The main severe threat, \nhowever should reside just to the east of our Coastal Plain, where \ninstability/shear is most favorable. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A secondary surge of strong wind gusts will develop \ntonight into early Friday.\n\nA secondary surge of near wind advisory criteria gusts is expected \nto develop tonight into early Friday. As the parent deep shortwave \ntrough over the MS/TN valley region this afternoon and evening \nswings into the Mid-Atlantic overnight into Friday, a surface cold \nfront will advance through. Strong winds aloft in the lowest few \nthousand feet will peak once again in the 35 to 40 kt range. \nForecast soundings indicate this could once again lead to strong \nwind gusts out of the southwest between 35 and 40 mph, with \ninfrequent gusts to 45 mph. Our current confidence is not high \nenough to issue another Wind Advisory, but later forecast shifts may \nneed to issue one for tonight into early Friday. Wind gusts Friday \nafternoon will remain elevated, but likely stay below advisory \ncriteria, in the 25-35 mph range before diminishing Friday night.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Turning warmer this weekend, followed by a chance \nof light rain late Sunday and Sunday night.\n\nThe Thanksgiving storm will move into the Canadian Maritimes/Western \nAtlantic this weekend, decreasing the pressure gradient and thus the \nwinds across central NC. Meanwhile, above-normal temperatures will \nreturn with SW flow ahead of the next mid-latitude cyclone that \ntracks from the Southern Plains NE into the MS and OH Valleys. \nForecast highs are in the upper 50s to lower 60s on Saturday and \nlower to mid 60s on Sunday. \n\nThe associated shortwave will swing east across the Appalachians and \nMid-Atlantic on Sunday night, as a cold front crosses central NC, \nwhich will provide the next decent chance of rain to the area. \nModels have come to a better consensus on the strength and track of \nthe low/shortwave, and overall timing has sped up a bit. While \nensemble agreement has increased that there will be some \nprecipitation on Sunday evening/night (90% of LREF members depict \nsomething measurable), rainfall amounts should be fairly light due \nto a lack of instability and the best forcing to our north. WPC QPF \nis bet",
